  "step_2": "The verbal report is in German and translates to: 'Lottery A has a higher probability of losing money.' The individual focuses on the probability of losing money and identifies that Lottery A involves a loss with certainty (100%), whereas Lottery B involves a loss only with 0.1% probability. The individual appears to consider the likelihood of incurring any monetary loss as a key factor. However, the justification seems to frame 'losing money' as the unfavorable outcome, and thus a higher probability of losing money would make Lottery A worse. This suggests the individual may be interpreting 'favorable outcome' as 'not losing money'. In that case, Lottery B would have a 99.9% chance of a favorable outcome (0 Euros, no loss), and Lottery A has 0% chance of avoiding a loss. But the individual states that A has a higher probability of losing money, which is correct, yet this would imply A is worse if avoiding loss is favorable. The justification is minimal and only points to this probabilistic comparison without clarifying whether this led to preferring A or B. Still, the focus is on the probability of a negative event (losing money), not on the magnitude of the loss.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the probability of a favorable outcome, treating outcome differences as negligible. It would define favorable outcome as avoiding the worst loss (i.e., not losing 5000 Euros), which both lotteries achieve with very high probability (100% for A, 99.9% for B), and thus prefers A due to slightly higher probability. However, the individual interprets 'favorable' as 'not losing any money', which would make the favorable outcome 0 Euros or better. Under that interpretation, B has a 99.9% chance of a favorable outcome (0 Euros), while A has 0%. Thus, the individual’s focus on the probability of losing money aligns with assessing the likelihood of a favorable (non-loss) outcome, but with a different definition of 'favorable' than implied by the reason. Moreover, the reason assumes outcome differences are negligible, but here the individual does not comment on outcome magnitudes at all—only on the probability of loss. The reasoning process overlaps partially in structure (focusing on probability of favorable outcome) but diverges in the definition of what constitutes a favorable outcome. The reason treats small and large losses as similar if they avoid the worst outcome, while the individual appears to treat any loss as unfavorable. Given this mismatch in outcome evaluation, despite a superficial similarity in using probability, it is uncertain whether the individual applied the specified decision reason. The core assumption of negligible outcome differences is not reflected in the report.",
  "final_assessment_score": 40
}

